Parameter:
  None.

Description:
  Changes the character set used for your terminal.

  SklaffKOM shows a small menu where you can choose between UTF-8,
  IBM CP437, ISO-8859-1, and classic SF7.

  After making your choice, a short test line with Swedish characters
  is displayed.

  If the characters look right, answer Yes. Your choice is saved and
  will be used automatically the next time you log in.

  If things look a little strange, answer No and you can try again.

Please note:
  UTF-8 is the best choice for most modern terminals, SSH sessions,
  and telnet clients.

  IBM CP437 is commonly used by classic BBS clients such as SyncTERM
  and NetRunner. ISO-8859-1 is often useful on systems such as Amiga.

  SF7 is SklaffKOM's classic internal character set and remains
  available for anyone wishing to keep one foot firmly in the 1990s.

  Only one character set is used at a time.

Background:
  SklaffKOM stores text internally using SF7 and translates it to the
  character set understood by your terminal.

  This command exists so you do not have to manage the individual
  character-set flags yourself.
